  {
    "episode_id": 91644,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91644.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 6,
    "turn_text": "It's certainly been interesting. But respectfully, I've noticed in your country, you've had some pretty good elections as well in New Zealand first. And the first go around, the second go around, and the third go around. The ups and downs in holding up government sitting and that sort of thing. So it happens. That's what happens when you have a process, a constitutional republic like ours, and they follow a democratic process to allow everyone to be heard. It was very messy. It's how our forefathers wanted it. to be those checks and balances. But yes, it's been entirely unusual to say the least. And I've certainly followed it very, very closely. Yeah, listen, if you told me that Kamala Harris would be where she is today, I would have said no way. She received no votes by any election. She actually dropped out with less than 2% when she ran for president. And during her administration, she has been basically nonexistent. Now, all of a sudden, she's going to be the so-called change agent. She's going to go and do all these amazing things, yet as Brett Bear asked her, well, you've been there three and a half years already. Like, why haven't you done it? And really, that's what the race is all about. People are very, very closely now looking as to how it was under President Trump. Well, we had low inflation. The border was pretty much getting secured. If not secured, the crossings were way, way down. Fenton always down. Mortgage rates were down. People had a lot of savings. They were the job creation and wages for minorities and others were were up dramatically. Energy was their energy independent for the first time in time, I think in my lifetime. And now we're not, you know, we've had over what 15, 16 million people coming in illegally. We don't even know who some of them are. They just let them go. That would never happen in a country like New Zealand ever. Yet here we are. And it's happened and it's putting a real strain on the economies of not only our states, but obviously our country as a whole. There are some cities that have put billions of dollars in giving housing and cash cards and phones to people that are here illegally, yet there are citizens, veterans and minorities and people that are struggling dramatically are getting nothing. And there's sort of a tremendous amount of resentment. So if you asked me three weeks ago who would win, I'd say it was probably the vice president. She probably was going to win. But her strategy has been a terrible one in my estimation of hiding. She has not done any real interviews, you know, spur of the moment in press conferences, any interview that she has done its only recently, they've been scripted and edited. And, you know, she basically is producing word salad, you know, she's using soundbites, she's using a teleprompter, you have Vance and Trump, they've had almost twice as many interviews, and they just do it for the moment, anywhere, ask, answer anything, anytime, and",
